-- Q1: hard / L4 — Foreign tax credit limitation calculation with excess carryforward
(3, 'Individual Taxation: Credits', 'Whitfield, a U.S. citizen, has worldwide taxable income of $250,000, including $75,000 of foreign-source general category income. Her U.S. tax liability before credits is $52,000. Whitfield paid $18,000 in foreign income taxes. Evaluate Whitfield''s foreign tax credit position and determine the allowable credit and any excess carryforward.', '["$15,600 credit with $2,400 carryforward", "$18,000 credit with no carryforward", "$15,600 credit with no carryforward", "$52,000 credit limited to foreign taxes paid"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under Section 904, the FTC limitation equals U.S. tax multiplied by the ratio of foreign-source income to worldwide income: $52,000 × ($75,000 / $250,000) = $15,600. Because Whitfield paid $18,000 in foreign taxes, the allowable credit is limited to $15,600, with the $2,400 excess carrying back one year or forward ten years under Section 904(c). Choice B incorrectly assumes the full amount paid is creditable without applying the limitation formula.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q2: medium / L2 — Saver''s Credit rate and maximum
(3, 'Individual Taxation: Credits', 'Torres, a single taxpayer age 30, contributes $3,000 to her traditional IRA in 2026. Her AGI is $21,000. Under Section 25B, what is Torres''s retirement savings contributions credit (Saver''s Credit)?', '["$1,500 (50% of $3,000)", "$1,000 (50% of $2,000 maximum)", "$600 (20% of $3,000)", "$200 (10% of $2,000 maximum)"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under Section 25B, the Saver''s Credit is a nonrefundable credit based on eligible retirement contributions up to $2,000 per individual. At an AGI of $21,000 (single), Torres qualifies for the 50% rate. The credit is 50% × $2,000 = $1,000. Choice A incorrectly applies the 50% rate to the full $3,000 contribution rather than the $2,000 statutory maximum per individual.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q3: medium / L3 — Child and dependent care credit percentage calculation
(3, 'Individual Taxation: Credits', 'Henderson, a single parent with AGI of $25,000, pays $4,200 in childcare expenses for her two children ages 4 and 7 to enable her to work full-time. Apply the child and dependent care credit rules under Section 21 to determine Henderson''s credit amount.', '["$840 (20% of $4,200)", "$1,200 (20% of $6,000)", "$1,260 (30% of $4,200)", "$1,800 (30% of $6,000)"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under Section 21, the maximum qualifying expenses for two or more individuals are $6,000. Henderson''s actual expenses of $4,200 fall below this cap. At AGI of $25,000, the applicable percentage is 30% (35% reduced by one percentage point for each $2,000 of AGI above $15,000). The credit is 30% × $4,200 = $1,260. Choice D incorrectly uses the $6,000 statutory cap rather than Henderson''s actual expenses of $4,200.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q4: medium / L3 — AMT SALT deduction added back
(3, 'Individual Taxation: Credits', 'Wagner itemizes deductions and claims $10,000 in state and local taxes on his regular return. His regular taxable income is $180,000. For purposes of computing Alternative Minimum Taxable Income (AMTI) under Section 56, how is Wagner''s SALT deduction treated?', '["The SALT deduction remains fully deductible for AMT", "Only the state income tax portion is added back for AMT", "Only amounts exceeding $10,000 are added back for AMT", "The entire $10,000 SALT deduction is added back to compute AMTI"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under Section 56(b)(1)(A), state and local tax deductions are entirely added back when computing AMTI. The SALT deduction is one of the most common AMT adjustments. Wagner must add the full $10,000 back to taxable income to arrive at AMTI. Choice A is incorrect because SALT is a disallowed deduction for AMT purposes regardless of the amount or type of state or local tax claimed.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q5: easy / L1 — Credits vs deductions value comparison
(3, 'Individual Taxation: Credits', 'How does a $1,000 tax credit compare to a $1,000 tax deduction for a taxpayer in the 22% bracket?', '["Both reduce tax liability by $1,000", "The deduction is more valuable than the credit", "Both reduce taxable income by $1,000", "The credit saves $1,000 while the deduction saves only $220"]'::jsonb, 3, 'A tax credit provides a dollar-for-dollar reduction in tax liability, so a $1,000 credit reduces tax owed by the full $1,000. A deduction reduces taxable income, so a $1,000 deduction in the 22% bracket saves only $220 (22% × $1,000). Credits are therefore more valuable than deductions of the same amount. Choice A is incorrect because only the credit reduces liability by $1,000; the deduction reduces it by just $220.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q6: easy / L1 — Foreign tax credit carryback and carryforward periods
(3, 'Individual Taxation: Credits', 'Under Section 904(c), what are the carryback and carryforward periods for unused foreign tax credits?', '["One year back and ten years forward", "No carryback and five years forward", "Two years back and twenty years forward", "Three years back and fifteen years forward"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under Section 904(c), foreign tax credits that exceed the limitation in the current year may be carried back one year and forward ten years. This allows taxpayers to use excess credits in years when their limitation is higher. Choice B is incorrect because unlike net operating losses under current law, the foreign tax credit retains its one-year carryback provision alongside the ten-year carryforward.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q7: medium / L2 — AOTC four-year limit
(3, 'Individual Taxation: Credits', 'Dawson claimed the American Opportunity Tax Credit for his son''s freshman, sophomore, junior, and senior years (2022-2025). In 2026, his son enrolls in a fifth year to complete a double major. Under Section 25A, can Dawson claim the AOTC for 2026?', '["Yes, because the student is still enrolled at least half-time", "Yes, because the fifth year qualifies if the student has not received a degree", "No, because the AOTC is limited to the first four taxable years of post-secondary education", "No, because the student must be under age 24 in the fifth year"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under Section 25A(b)(2)(A), the AOTC is available only for the first four taxable years of post-secondary education. Since Dawson already claimed the credit for four years (2022-2025), his son is ineligible for 2026 regardless of enrollment status or degree completion. The Lifetime Learning Credit may still be available. Choice A is incorrect because half-time enrollment alone does not override the four-year statutory limit.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q8: medium / L3 — AMT private activity bond interest as preference item
(3, 'Individual Taxation: Credits', 'Morrison holds $200,000 in private activity municipal bonds generating $8,000 in tax-exempt interest during 2026. He also holds $150,000 in general obligation municipal bonds paying $5,500 in interest. Apply Section 57(a)(5) to determine the AMT treatment of Morrison''s bond interest.', '["Both types of bond interest ($13,500 total) are added back for AMT", "Only the $8,000 private activity bond interest is an AMT preference item", "Only the $5,500 general obligation bond interest is added back for AMT", "Neither type of bond interest affects the AMT calculation"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under Section 57(a)(5), interest on specified private activity bonds is a tax preference item that must be added back when computing AMTI. The $8,000 from private activity bonds is included in AMTI. General obligation municipal bond interest remains tax-exempt for both regular tax and AMT purposes. Choice A is incorrect because general obligation bond interest is not a preference item and does not increase AMTI.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q9: hard / L4 — AMT exemption phase-out calculation
(3, 'Individual Taxation: Credits', 'Pratt, a single taxpayer, computes AMTI of $720,000 for 2026. The AMT exemption for single filers is $88,000, and the phase-out begins at $609,000. Evaluate the impact of the exemption phase-out and determine Pratt''s remaining AMT exemption amount.', '["$88,000 (no phase-out applies)", "$0 (fully phased out)", "$27,750 (the phase-out amount replaces the exemption)", "$60,250 (exemption reduced by 25% of excess AMTI over threshold)"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under Section 55(d)(3), the AMT exemption phases out at 25 cents per dollar of AMTI above the threshold. Pratt''s excess AMTI is $720,000 − $609,000 = $111,000. The phase-out reduction is $111,000 × 25% = $27,750. The remaining exemption is $88,000 − $27,750 = $60,250. Choice B is incorrect because full phase-out requires AMTI of $609,000 + ($88,000 × 4) = $961,000, which Pratt has not reached.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q10: hard / L4 — Estimated tax de minimis exception overrides other rules
(3, 'Individual Taxation: Credits', 'Bradley, a self-employed taxpayer with prior-year AGI of $175,000, withholds nothing and makes no estimated payments during 2026. Her total tax liability after credits is $800. Evaluate whether Bradley owes an underpayment penalty under Section 6654 and identify the applicable exception.', '["Penalty applies because she made no estimated payments on prior-year AGI over $150,000", "Penalty applies because self-employed taxpayers must always make estimated payments", "No penalty because her prior-year AGI was under $200,000", "No penalty because the total tax due after withholding is less than $1,000"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under Section 6654(e)(1), no underpayment penalty applies if the total tax owed after withholding and credits is less than $1,000. Bradley''s balance due of $800 falls below this de minimis threshold, so no penalty applies regardless of her income level or failure to make estimated payments. Choice A is incorrect because the $150,000 AGI threshold affects the safe harbor percentage (100% vs. 110%) but the $1,000 de minimis exception overrides the penalty entirely.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q11: easy / L1 — Estimated tax quarterly due dates
(3, 'Individual Taxation: Credits', 'What are the quarterly due dates for individual estimated tax payments?', '["March 15, June 15, September 15, December 15", "January 15, April 15, July 15, October 15", "April 15, June 15, September 15, January 15", "April 15, July 15, October 15, January 15"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under Section 6654(c), estimated tax payments for individuals are due on April 15, June 15, September 15 of the current year, and January 15 of the following year. The quarters are not evenly spaced—the second installment is due only two months after the first. Choice D is incorrect because it assumes evenly spaced quarterly intervals, but the second payment is due June 15, not July 15.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q12: hard / L3 — Lifetime Learning Credit per-return cap with multiple students
(3, 'Individual Taxation: Credits', 'Grant and Elena (MFJ) have two children in college in 2026. They pay $7,000 in qualified tuition for their son and $5,000 for their daughter. Their MAGI is $75,000. Neither child is in the first four years. Apply the Lifetime Learning Credit rules under Section 25A(c) to determine their maximum credit.', '["$4,000 (20% of $12,000 combined expenses, per-student basis)", "$2,000 (20% of the $10,000 per-return cap)", "$2,400 (20% of $12,000 without regard to the cap)", "$1,400 (20% of $7,000 for the higher-expense student only)"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under Section 25A(c), the Lifetime Learning Credit is computed per return, not per student. The maximum qualified expenses considered are $10,000 per return, yielding a maximum credit of 20% × $10,000 = $2,000. Although combined expenses total $12,000, only $10,000 counts toward the calculation. Choice A is incorrect because treating the LLC as per-student confuses it with the AOTC structure.', 'hard', 3),

-- Q13: medium / L3 — Foreign tax credit vs deduction comparison
(3, 'Individual Taxation: Credits', 'Lin, a single taxpayer in the 32% bracket, pays $3,000 in foreign income taxes on investment income in 2026. Her foreign tax credit limitation exceeds $3,000. Apply the interaction between Section 901 (credit) and Section 164 (deduction) to determine the better tax treatment.', '["Claim the $3,000 as a credit, saving $3,000 in U.S. tax", "Claim the $3,000 as an itemized deduction, saving $3,000 in U.S. tax", "Claim $1,500 as a credit and $1,500 as a deduction to split the benefit", "The credit and deduction produce identical tax savings"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under Section 901, the foreign tax credit provides a dollar-for-dollar reduction in U.S. tax liability. Since Lin''s limitation exceeds the $3,000 paid, the full amount offsets U.S. tax. Alternatively, Section 164 allows a deduction saving only 32% × $3,000 = $960. A taxpayer cannot claim both a credit and deduction for the same foreign taxes. Choice B is incorrect because a deduction reduces taxable income, not tax liability, producing a smaller benefit than the credit.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q14: hard / L4 — AMT calculation with ISO spread
(3, 'Individual Taxation: Credits', 'Chen exercises incentive stock options in 2026, purchasing 2,000 shares at $15 per share when the FMV is $35 per share. His regular taxable income is $200,000 producing regular tax of $38,000. The ISO spread is the only AMT adjustment. The single-filer AMT exemption is $88,000. Evaluate Chen''s AMT exposure.', '["$0 because ISO exercises are not taxable for regular tax purposes", "$40,000 because the full ISO spread is subject to AMT at ordinary rates", "$39,520 tentative minimum tax with no additional AMT owed", "$39,520 tentative minimum tax, resulting in $1,520 of additional AMT"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under Section 56(b)(3), the ISO spread ($35 − $15) × 2,000 = $40,000 is added to regular taxable income to compute AMTI of $240,000. After subtracting the $88,000 exemption, the AMT base of $152,000 is taxed at 26%, yielding a tentative minimum tax of $39,520. Since this exceeds the $38,000 regular tax, Chen owes additional AMT of $1,520. Choice A correctly notes ISOs create no regular tax but incorrectly concludes there is no AMT impact.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q15: medium / L3 — AOTC half-time enrollment requirement
(3, 'Individual Taxation: Credits', 'Palmer takes one evening course at a community college in 2026, paying $1,800 in tuition. She is not enrolled at least half-time. Her MAGI is $50,000 (single) and she has never claimed the AOTC. Apply the education credit rules under Section 25A to determine which credit Palmer may claim.', '["AOTC of $1,800 since she has not reached the four-year limit", "AOTC of $1,000 (the 40% refundable portion only)", "Lifetime Learning Credit of up to $360 (20% of $1,800)", "No education credit is available because she takes only one course"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under Section 25A(b)(2)(B), the AOTC requires enrollment at least half-time in a degree or credential program. Palmer is not half-time, disqualifying her from the AOTC. However, the Lifetime Learning Credit under Section 25A(c) has no minimum enrollment requirement and is available for one or more courses. Her LLC is 20% × $1,800 = $360. Choice A is incorrect because half-time enrollment is mandatory for AOTC eligibility.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q16: easy / L1 — $500 Other Dependent Credit
(3, 'Individual Taxation: Credits', 'What credit is available for a dependent who does not qualify for the $2,000 child tax credit?', '["No credit is available for other dependents", "A $500 nonrefundable credit for other dependents", "A $500 refundable credit for other dependents", "A $1,000 nonrefundable credit for other dependents"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under Section 24(h)(4), a $500 nonrefundable credit is available for dependents who do not qualify for the full child tax credit, such as children age 17 or older and dependent parents. This credit cannot generate a refund and has the same phase-out thresholds as the CTC ($200,000 single / $400,000 MFJ). Choice C is incorrect because the other dependent credit is nonrefundable, unlike the partially refundable child tax credit.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q17: medium / L3 — Estimated tax zero prior-year liability exception
(3, 'Individual Taxation: Credits', 'Kowalski, a U.S. citizen and resident for all of 2025, had zero tax liability in 2025 due to large business losses. In 2026, she earns $300,000 and makes no estimated payments. Her 2026 liability is $70,000. Apply the penalty exception rules under Section 6654(e) to determine whether Kowalski owes an underpayment penalty.', '["Penalty applies because 2026 liability exceeds $1,000 and she made no payments", "Penalty applies because she must use the 110% prior-year safe harbor", "Penalty applies because the zero-liability exception requires AGI under $150,000", "No penalty because her prior-year tax liability was zero and she was a U.S. citizen/resident for the entire year"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under Section 6654(e)(2), no underpayment penalty applies if the taxpayer had zero tax liability in the prior year and was a U.S. citizen or resident for the entire prior year. Because Kowalski''s 2025 tax was zero and she was a citizen/resident all year, the exception eliminates any 2026 penalty regardless of current-year income. Choice A is incorrect because the zero-liability exception overrides the general penalty rules.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q18: medium / L2 — AMT two-bracket rate structure
(3, 'Individual Taxation: Credits', 'Rivera computes an AMT base (AMTI minus exemption) of $300,000 for 2026. Under Section 55(b), the AMT applies a 26% rate on the first $232,600 and 28% on amounts above. What is Rivera''s tentative minimum tax?', '["$79,348", "$78,000 (26% of $300,000)", "$84,000 (28% of $300,000)", "$60,476 (26% of $232,600 only)"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under Section 55(b)(1), the AMT uses a two-bracket rate structure: 26% on the first $232,600 and 28% on amounts above. Rivera''s TMT is (26% × $232,600) + (28% × $67,400) = $60,476 + $18,872 = $79,348. Choice B incorrectly applies a flat 26% rate to the entire AMT base, ignoring the 28% bracket that applies to the $67,400 exceeding the $232,600 threshold.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q19: medium / L2 — EIC phase-in structure
(3, 'Individual Taxation: Credits', 'Hoffman, a single taxpayer with two qualifying children, earns $8,000 in wages in 2026 with no other income. The EIC phase-in rate for two qualifying children is approximately 40%. Under Section 32, how does the credit relate to Hoffman''s earned income?', '["The credit is fixed at the maximum regardless of income level", "The credit decreases as earned income increases during the phase-in range", "The credit increases with earned income during the phase-in range (approximately 40% × $8,000 = $3,200)", "The credit equals 40% of AGI rather than earned income"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under Section 32(a), the EIC during the phase-in range equals the credit percentage multiplied by earned income. With two qualifying children at approximately 40%, Hoffman''s credit is about $3,200. The credit increases as earned income rises until reaching the maximum, plateaus, then phases out at higher income levels. Choice B reverses the relationship—the credit grows with income during the phase-in range, not decreases.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q20: easy / L1 — Adoption credit is nonrefundable with carryforward
(3, 'Individual Taxation: Credits', 'Is the adoption credit under Section 23 refundable or nonrefundable?', '["Fully refundable", "Nonrefundable, with unused amounts carried forward up to five years", "Partially refundable at 40%", "Nonrefundable with no carryforward"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under Section 23, the adoption credit is nonrefundable, meaning it can reduce tax liability to zero but cannot produce a refund. However, unused adoption credits may be carried forward for up to five years under Section 23(c). Choice A is incorrect because the adoption credit is explicitly nonrefundable, unlike credits such as the EIC or the refundable portion of the AOTC.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q21: medium / L2 — Child and dependent care qualifying child age limit
(3, 'Individual Taxation: Credits', 'Mitchell pays $5,000 in after-school care costs for his daughter age 14 and $3,500 for his son age 11 in 2026 while he and his spouse work full-time. Under Section 21, which child''s expenses qualify for the child and dependent care credit?', '["Both children qualify because both are under age 16", "Both children qualify because both are dependents on the return", "Neither child qualifies because the credit is only for children under age 6", "Only the 11-year-old son''s expenses qualify because the child must be under age 13"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under Section 21(b)(1), the child and dependent care credit requires the qualifying individual to be a dependent under age 13. Mitchell''s son (age 11) qualifies, but his daughter (age 14) exceeds the age limit. The $3,500 in expenses for the son is eligible, subject to applicable percentage and dollar limits. Choice A is incorrect because the statutory age cutoff is under 13, not under 16.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q22: medium / L2 — Foreign tax credit Form 1116 de minimis election
(3, 'Individual Taxation: Credits', 'Brooks and Taylor (MFJ) receive $450 in foreign tax withheld on dividends from an international mutual fund in 2026. Their only foreign-source income is passive category dividends. Under Section 904, must they file Form 1116 to claim the foreign tax credit?', '["No, because MFJ taxpayers with $600 or less in creditable foreign taxes from passive income only may claim the credit directly on Form 1040", "No, because Form 1116 is never required for amounts under $1,000", "Yes, because all foreign tax credits require Form 1116 regardless of amount", "Yes, because passive category income always requires separate Form 1116 reporting"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under Section 904(j), married taxpayers filing jointly with $600 or less ($300 single) in creditable foreign taxes from passive category income only may elect to claim the credit directly on Form 1040 without filing Form 1116. Brooks and Taylor''s $450 qualifies for this simplified election. Choice C is incorrect because Congress provided this de minimis exception specifically to reduce compliance burden for small foreign tax amounts.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q23: medium / L3 — Additional Child Tax Credit refundable portion with earned income threshold
(3, 'Individual Taxation: Credits', 'Franklin has one qualifying child under age 17 in 2026. Her tax liability before credits is $0 due to other nonrefundable credits, and her earned income is $25,000. Apply the Additional Child Tax Credit rules to determine the refundable portion of her child tax credit.', '["$1,700 (the maximum refundable ACTC, since 15% of excess earned income exceeds the cap)", "$2,000 (the full unused CTC becomes refundable)", "$3,375 (15% of earned income above $2,500)", "$800 (the standard refundable amount for one child)"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under Section 24(d), the ACTC equals the lesser of the unused CTC or 15% of earned income above $2,500, capped at $1,700 per child. Franklin''s unused CTC is $2,000, and her earned income formula yields 15% × ($25,000 − $2,500) = $3,375, but the refundable portion is capped at $1,700. Choice B is incorrect because the full $2,000 CTC is not entirely refundable—only up to $1,700 may be refunded as the ACTC.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q24: hard / L4 — Multiple credits ordering and interaction
(3, 'Individual Taxation: Credits', 'Douglas has a pre-credit tax liability of $3,000 in 2026. He qualifies for a $2,000 child tax credit (up to $1,700 refundable), a $2,500 child and dependent care credit (nonrefundable), and a $4,000 earned income credit (fully refundable). Evaluate the credit ordering rules and determine Douglas''s final refund amount.', '["$4,000 refund (only the EIC is refundable)", "$5,000 refund ($3,000 excess nonrefundable credits plus $4,000 EIC minus $2,000)", "$5,500 refund (nonrefundable credits reduce tax to zero; then $1,500 ACTC plus $4,000 EIC)", "$4,500 refund ($500 unused nonrefundable credits are lost)"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Nonrefundable credits apply first: the $2,500 care credit reduces tax from $3,000 to $500. Then $500 of the $2,000 CTC reduces tax to zero, leaving $1,500 unused CTC that becomes a $1,500 ACTC (under the $1,700 cap). Refundable credits then generate a refund: $1,500 ACTC + $4,000 EIC = $5,500 total refund. Choice A incorrectly ignores the ACTC, treating the entire unused CTC as a lost nonrefundable credit.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q25: medium / L2 — AMT standard deduction added back
(3, 'Individual Taxation: Credits', 'Yamamoto, a single taxpayer, claims the $15,000 standard deduction on his 2026 regular tax return instead of itemizing. Under Section 56, how is the standard deduction treated for AMT purposes?', '["The standard deduction is allowed for both regular tax and AMT", "The standard deduction must be added back to compute AMTI", "Only the portion exceeding $10,000 is added back for AMT", "The standard deduction is replaced by a separate AMT standard deduction"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under Section 56(b)(1)(E), the standard deduction is not allowed for AMT purposes and must be added back when computing Alternative Minimum Taxable Income. This means taxpayers who claim the standard deduction have a higher starting point for AMTI than their regular taxable income. Choice A is incorrect because the standard deduction is one of the adjustments that differentiates AMT from the regular tax calculation.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q26: easy / L2 — EIC maximum for no qualifying children
(3, 'Individual Taxation: Credits', 'Williams, a single taxpayer age 28 with no qualifying children, has earned income of $9,000 and AGI below the applicable threshold in 2026. Under Section 32, what is the approximate maximum earned income credit for a taxpayer with no qualifying children?', '["$2,500", "$4,200", "$1,000", "Approximately $650"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under Section 32, the maximum EIC for a taxpayer with no qualifying children is approximately $650 (indexed annually for inflation). This is significantly less than the maximum for taxpayers with children, which ranges from about $4,200 (one child) to $7,800 (three or more children). Choice B incorrectly states the maximum for one qualifying child rather than zero qualifying children.', 'easy', 2),

-- Q27: easy / L1 — Nonrefundable credit definition
(3, 'Individual Taxation: Credits', 'What is a nonrefundable tax credit?', '["A credit that can reduce tax liability to zero but cannot generate a refund", "A credit that can both reduce tax liability and generate a cash refund", "A credit that reduces taxable income rather than tax liability", "A credit available only to taxpayers who itemize deductions"]'::jsonb, 0, 'A nonrefundable tax credit reduces a taxpayer''s tax liability dollar-for-dollar but cannot reduce it below zero. Any excess credit beyond the tax owed is generally lost unless a carryforward provision applies. Examples include the child and dependent care credit and the Lifetime Learning Credit. Choice B describes a refundable credit, such as the EIC, which can produce a cash refund to the taxpayer.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q28: easy / L1 — LLC unlimited number of years
(3, 'Individual Taxation: Credits', 'How many years can a taxpayer claim the Lifetime Learning Credit under Section 25A(c)?', '["First four years of post-secondary education only", "First two years of graduate school only", "An unlimited number of years", "A maximum of six years total"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under Section 25A(c), the Lifetime Learning Credit has no limit on the number of years it can be claimed. It is available for undergraduate, graduate, and professional development courses indefinitely. This contrasts with the AOTC, which is limited to the first four taxable years of post-secondary education under Section 25A(b)(2)(A). Choice A describes the AOTC''s four-year limitation, not the LLC.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q29: medium / L2 — Estimated tax 100% vs 110% prior-year safe harbor
(3, 'Individual Taxation: Credits', 'Reed had prior-year AGI of $140,000 and prior-year tax liability of $28,000. His 2026 tax liability is $42,000. Under Section 6654, what is the minimum amount Reed must pay through withholding and estimated payments to avoid the underpayment penalty using the prior-year safe harbor?', '["$28,000 (100% of prior-year tax, since prior-year AGI is $150,000 or less)", "$30,800 (110% of prior-year tax)", "$37,800 (90% of current-year tax)", "$42,000 (100% of current-year tax)"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under Section 6654(d)(1)(B), the prior-year safe harbor requires payment of 100% of the prior-year tax liability when prior-year AGI is $150,000 or less. Since Reed''s prior-year AGI of $140,000 is below the threshold, he must pay only $28,000 to avoid the penalty. Choice B incorrectly applies the 110% safe harbor, which is required only when prior-year AGI exceeds $150,000.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q30: easy / L1 — AMT preference items definition
(3, 'Individual Taxation: Credits', 'What are AMT preference items under Section 57?', '["Deductions that may either increase or decrease AMTI", "Items that are always added back to taxable income, increasing AMTI", "Credits that are disallowed for AMT purposes", "Income exclusions that reduce both regular tax and AMT equally"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under Section 57, AMT preference items are specific tax benefits that must always be added to taxable income when computing Alternative Minimum Taxable Income, such as private activity bond interest and excess intangible drilling costs. Unlike AMT adjustments under Section 56, which can increase or decrease AMTI, preferences only increase it. Choice A describes AMT adjustments, not preference items.', 'easy', 1)
;

COMMIT;
